---
import CaseStudy from "@/layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import { projects } from "@/data/projects";

const project = projects.find((p) => p.slug === "miw-architects")!;
---

<CaseStudy project={project}>
  <div class="prose-case mt-20">
    <h2>The practice</h2>
    <p>
      MIW works across commercial, residential, and mixed-use development from Chicago, with projects
      reaching New York and Seoul. Restaurant design is a particular specialty — the studio runs market
      research and feasibility studies and assembles investor-facing presentation packages alongside the
      architecture itself. On the residential side it's mostly renovation and new construction, with a
      high proportion of returning clients.
    </p>

    <h2>The brief</h2>
    <p>
      An architecture studio's site lives or dies on one question: whether the photography and the
      buildings are allowed to do the talking. The work is the entire pitch. The interface's job is to
      present it cleanly and then get out of the way.
    </p>

    <h2>What I built</h2>
    <p>
      A gallery-first studio site organized around the categories MIW actually sells against — commercial
      (restaurant, cafe, spa, daycare, office), residential (new construction, rehab, renovation),
      development (mixed-use, multi-family), and custom artwork and fabrication. The projects page opens on
      a reel of built work in motion before resolving into stills. Adding a finished project is a content
      change, not a layout change, so the gallery grows without a developer in the loop.
    </p>
  </div>
</CaseStudy>
